  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure currently centered over western Ontario will be \nbuilding into the area later today leading to drier, less humid\nand quiet conditions into Saturday. Lingering clouds and a \ncouple of brief showers or sprinkles around early this morning \nin the wake of a cold frontal passage will give way to clearing\nand a mostly sunny afternoon. Dew points will be falling as \nwell so it won't feel nearly as sticky outside. High \ntemperatures across CNY are expected to be a few degrees lower \nthan Thursday, but overall pretty close to average in the upper \n60s and low 70s. Highs are expected to be in the mid and upper \n70s in portions of NE PA and Sullivan County, NY, mainly in the \nWyoming and Delaware River Valleys. As high pressure settles \noverhead tonight with light winds and a clear to partly cloudy \nsky, good radiational cooling conditions will be in place. Lows \nwill be largely in the 40s with some spots in the Catskills and \nNorthern Oneida County dropping into the upper 30s. Patchy \nfrost cannot be ruled out in these locations, especially if lows\ndrop a couple of degrees lower, but confidence remains low at \nthis time to add to the forecast grids. \n\nOne more dry day is expected on Saturday with a good deal of \nsunshine, although some higher clouds may sneak in from the west\nlater in the day ahead of the next system as high pressure \nslides to the east. Highs on Saturday are expected to be in the \nmid 60s to lower 70s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nA low pressure system will approach from the west later \nSaturday night into Sunday, bringing the next round of rain to \nthe area. Southwesterly flow ahead of the low will transport \nmoisture northward, supporting the potential for a widespread \nrainfall. Rain is expected through much of Sunday before \ngradually tapering off by Sunday evening. There is the potential\nfor some areas of locally heavy rainfall, especially across \nportions of NE PA and the Southern Catskills Sunday. PWATs climb\nto 1.50 to 1.80 inches or so on average with warm cloud layer \ndepths of 11-12K feet and strengthening winds aloft as winds at\n850mb increase across central and eastern PA, the Southern Tier\nand into the Catskills. The NAM has winds as high as 45 to 55 \nknots, while the GFS and Euro don't look to be as bullish, but \nstill the ingredients are there for a soaking rainfall. Most \ndeterministic and ensemble model guidance have rainfall amounts \ntopping out around 1.25-1.50 inches of rain late Saturday night \nthrough Sunday evening, but there can be a little enhancement \ndue to the ingredients just mentioned. However, the NBM looked \ntoo high in it's rainfall totals of 2.20 to nearly 2.50 inches \nin parts of this area, especially given the lack of instability \nand convection, so WPC was blended in to taper those numbers a \nbit. This gives us rainfall totals of about 1.50-2.00 inches \nacross NE PA into the Southern Catskills, about 0.50 to 1.25 \ninches for the Southern Tier of NY and less than 0.50 inch of \nrain around the Finger Lakes northeastward toward the Tug Hill \nregion. Sunday will be a noticeably cooler day with highs in the\nupper 50s to the mid 60s. \n\nMonday currently looks mainly dry as surface high pressure \ndevelops north of the Great Lakes. That being said, some \nlingering showers will be possible in Northeast PA.\n\nTuesday through at least midweek there appears to be a cutoff \nupper level low that develops near the Great Lakes that tries to\ndrift toward our area. This would lead to cloudy, cool and \nunsettled conditions with rounds of showers and below-average \ntemperatures through Thursday.",
